## What we want when modelling? {.smaller}

::: {style="margin-top: 3.5em;"}

- Models that are easy to inspect and modify 
- Thousands of simulations and scenarios 
- Simulation, fitting and intervention analysis 
- Analyses that can be reproduced 

:::

::: {.notes}

:::

## Can't we do the same in other languages? {.smaller}

::: {style="margin-top: 1.5em;"}
Yes you can. Comparable models can be built, simulated and fitted in R, Python and
elsewhere. The methods we show here are not unique to Julia.

What Julia brings together:

- Model code can remain generic. Multiple dispatch selects an applicable method from the argument types, and those types give the compiler information it can use to optimise execution.
- Julia’s scientific packages make use of shared types and interfaces. This often allows a problem specification to be reused on different workflows.

Together, these features can mean less duplicated model code and less
framework-specific integration code when moving between stages of an analysis.

:::
::: {.notes}
Julia becomes particularly attractive when we are developing a custom model. For example, the code describing transmission can remain readable Julia code, while Julia compiles it for efficient execution. We can then connect that model to simulation, fitting, uncertainty analysis and intervention design without maintaining a separate low-level implementation.
:::

