## Reproducible randomness {.smaller}

::: {style="margin-top: 2.5em;"}

::: {#s-p01-rng .cell execution_count=1}
``` {.julia .cell-code}
using StableRNGs

a = rand(StableRNG(123), 3)   # a new generator, seed 123, returns three numbers
b = rand(StableRNG(123), 2)   # another new generator, same seed returns two numbers

rng = StableRNG(123)          # one generator, used twice
c = rand(rng, 3)
d = rand(rng, 3)

println("a, new generator:   ", round.(a; digits = 3))
println("b, new generator:   ", round.(b; digits = 3))
println("c, rng first call:  ", round.(c; digits = 3))
println("d, rng second call: ", round.(d; digits = 3))
```

::: {.cell-output .cell-output-stdout}
```
a, new generator:   [0.181, 0.367, 0.669]
b, new generator:   [0.181, 0.367]
c, rng first call:  [0.181, 0.367, 0.669]
d, rng second call: [0.043, 0.438, 0.483]
```
:::
:::



- A random-number generator produces a sequence, and the seed says where that sequence starts
- `a`, `b` and `c` each start a fresh sequence from seed 123, so they agree number for number — `b` only asked for the first two of them
- `d` reuses `rng`, which has already been advanced by the call that produced `c`, so it continues the sequence rather than repeating it
- Passing the generator as an argument keeps the randomness visible at the call site
:::

::: {.notes}
Read the four lines down the screen. The first three are separate sequences that
all start from seed 123, so they agree wherever they overlap; `b` stops after two
numbers only because that is what it asked for. The fourth is that same sequence
carried on from where `c` left it.

The contrast worth drawing is with a global seed. `StableRNGs` fixes the stream
across Julia versions, where the built-in generator may change between releases.
Passing the generator as an argument also makes the source of randomness visible
at the call site instead of depending on whatever ran before it.
:::

