## You do not have to leave R {.smaller}

| | |
|---|---|
| `RCall.jl` | `R"..."` runs R; `@rget`, `@rput` move variables |
| `PythonCall.jl` | Python from Julia, and `juliacall` for the reverse |
| `JuliaCall` (R package) | call your Julia model from R |
| `ccall` | a C or Fortran library directly, with a Julia wrapper |

- Adoption can be partial: one model kernel in Julia, called from the pipeline
  you already have.
- Each crossing converts data, and you now maintain two environments.

::: {.notes}
For this room this is often the slide that decides whether Julia is usable at
all, because nobody is going to re-implement a working surveillance pipeline.

Say plainly that the costs are real. Conversion at every crossing makes a loop
that calls R once per iteration slow, two package environments is the most common
thing to break, and an error on the far side arrives with a stack trace from the
other language.

`ccall` is worth one sentence beyond the table: reaching a C library takes no
hand-written C glue and no compilation step of your own, which is how a package
such as `Sundials.jl` gets at an established solver — it is a Julia wrapper
around `ccall`s.

None of these packages is installed for this course, so this slide is a pointer
rather than something to try today.
:::

